  <section class="section shell case-study" aria-labelledby="practice-title">
    <div class="case-kicker"><span>Ongoing practice</span><span>Systems and organizations</span></div>
    <div class="case-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 id="practice-title">The work beneath the work.</h2>
        <p class="case-lede">The recurring problems are rarely confined to code. They sit between architecture, ownership, customer trust, and the way decisions get made.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Dependable systems</h3>
        <p>How do we make complex systems observable, operable, and understandable—not only when they work, but when assumptions begin to fail?</p>
        <h3>Engineering organizations</h3>
        <p>How should technical boundaries and team boundaries reinforce each other? Where does coordination create safety, and where does it merely create waiting?</p>
        <h3>Product judgment</h3>
        <p>How do we connect deep technical choices to a customer problem without simplifying either side of the conversation?</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell timeline" aria-labelledby="themes-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Themes</p><h2 id="themes-title">What I keep returning to.</h2></div></div>
    <article><span>01 / Systems</span><h3>Reliability creates room for ambition</h3><p>Strong foundations are not a tax on innovation. They are what let teams make consequential changes without relying on hope as an operating model.</p></article>
    <article><span>02 / Teams</span><h3>Architecture distributes decisions</h3><p>Every boundary shapes who can act independently, where coordination happens, and how quickly an organization can learn.</p></article>
    <article><span>03 / Products</span><h3>Small experiments expose assumptions</h3><p>Building something modest and putting it in front of people is often the shortest route from an attractive theory to useful evidence.</p></article>
  </section>

